{
    "name": "Ossprey",
    "customizations": {
        "vscode": {
            "extensions": [
                "GitHub.copilot",
                "aaron-bond.better-comments",
                "github.vscode-github-actions",
                "timonwong.shellcheck",
                "foxundermoon.shell-format"
            ]
        }
    },
    /* No `image` here on purpose. This repository is public, so it cannot use
       ghcr.io/ossprey/devcontainer -- that package is private and an outside
       contributor's codespace could not pull it. The base image stays whatever
       the platform provides by default, and postCreateCommand installs the
       Ossprey CLI from the ossprey-cli releases, which are public.

       .devcontainer/install-ossprey-cli.sh is a VERBATIM COPY of
       scripts/install-ossprey-cli.sh in ossprey/devcontainer -- byte for byte,
       so `sha256sum` on the two is the drift check. Fix bugs there and re-copy;
       do not edit this repo's copy in place.

       Report-only, matching the shared image: an install is scanned but never
       blocked, so a container nobody has logged into still works. Run
       `ossprey login` once for findings to reach the dashboard; clear
       OSSPREY_CI_CACHE_SCAN_ONLY to enforce.

       Both Ossprey pins are required and move together. The tag is pinned
       rather than `latest` because the installer runs as root, so an unpinned
       URL would execute whatever it served at the time. The digest is pinned
       too because GitHub release assets are MUTABLE: the install.sh behind a
       fixed tag can be deleted and re-uploaded with different content. The
       script verifies the bytes before executing them and refuses to run
       without either pin, so bump both together -- copy the values from
       ossprey/devcontainer's Dockerfile. */
    "containerEnv": {
        "OSSPREY_CLI_VERSION": "v0.17.0",
        "OSSPREY_CLI_SHA256": "bb3203c44b4ea50cceb91f8edcea8574be763e85030380206140e4d02cd604e8",
        "OSSPREY_SHIM_DIR": "/usr/local/ossprey/shims",
        "OSSPREY_CI_CACHE_SCAN_ONLY": "1"
    },
    /* Load bearing, not leftover: the script installs the shims with
       --no-path, and containerEnv is layered under remoteEnv, so anything a
       devcontainer feature prepends to PATH would otherwise sit ahead of the
       shims -- CLI installed, nothing intercepted. `ossprey shim status` is
       the check. */
    "remoteEnv": {
        "PATH": "/usr/local/ossprey/shims:${containerEnv:PATH}"
    },
    "postCreateCommand": "bash .devcontainer/install-ossprey-cli.sh",
    "postStartCommand": "bash .devcontainer/startup.sh"
}
